# port-patches.sh: glm/series @ ee578fd4237f over glm/zig-engine-base @ 0db3e2f6652b (commits e4fb5b76cb1b..f139cada683a); regenerate with scripts/port-patches.sh, do not edit
core prompt cache + lane host: spill v2's Tier, restore jobs and the restore service beside decoding (port of GLM 0110's host side)

The prompt cache's half of spill v2, re-implemented on v1.0.7's store and lane
host (the DeepSeek port's tier, restore jobs and service did not carry over:
v1.0.7 rewrote both).

- prompt_cache: a Tier interface a family's spill tier implements; every
  kept state is written when it is kept (keepWith enqueues it, free stages
  or cancels it first); probe finds memory's best state and a longer durable
  point; restore jobs (one per point, coalesced, their destinations charged
  to the budget); adopt turns a finished restore into an ordinary kept
  state, replacing a stale memory twin of its point; a restore refused for
  capacity is deferrable. With no tier set the store is as before, and v1
  spill (Mia's cuda_spill, the rollback) is off whenever a tier is set.
- lane_host: a request whose prompt a disk point serves better than memory
  parks on its restore (no lane held), and the engine thread services the
  restores between rounds under one copy budget; a refused restore waits and
  asks again (at most TF_SPILL_DEFER_MS of serviceable time); the tier is
  flushed at a clean stop; the cache's and every rank's tier counters on
  /health (prompt_cache, prompt_cache.spill) and /metrics
  (tensorfold:prompt_cache_*, tensorfold:spill_*{rank="N"}).
- Two restores at once each restore from disk (round 2's dual-restore case
  on the pair: one restored, the other prefilled from 0 beside it and
  stalled a decoding stream 18.5 s): a restore refused while others are in
  flight waits for them and asks again as each ends, never a prefill from 0
  beside them; while requests are parked an admission runs one whole prompt
  pass and a long cold pass (8,192 tokens or more past memory's best state)
  waits for the parked restores, bounded by the same deferral.
- server: api.Spill and the spill v2 settings, read only for a GLM with the
  disk tier asked for (--spill-gib above 0 and --snapshot-dir).

Switches (rank 0): TF_SPILL_DEFER_MS (5000 ms; 0: nothing waits),
TF_GLM_SPILL_LOAD_MS (one service's budget for every restore, 40 ms),
TF_SPILL_LOAD_WAIT_MS (how long a request waits parked, 30000 ms),
--spill-flush-s (a clean stop's drain bound, 60 s), and spill v2's own
(TF_GLM_SPILL_QUOTA 0, _TURNS 4, _READ_BLOCKS 16, _STAGE_MIB 256), which the
GLM tier uses. Nothing here acts until a family sets a tier (the spill blocks
patch): off, every request and reply is as before.

Tests: the store side over a fake tier (write at keep, probe, restore jobs,
adopt over a stale twin, deferral, clear under a tier) and the lane host over
a fake tier and a stepped backend (parking, the service's budget, deferral and
its bound, shutdown flush, the counters), each failing without its fix; g2's
dual-restore reproduced on the host first.

Measured on the pair: with the GLM tier in round 2's image (g2), spill_check's
main cases 20/22 and its restart cases 4/4 passed; the dual-restore case
failed there and is what the concurrent-restore rules above fix (host-tested;
not yet run on the pair).

Credits: Robert Wojciechowski (GitHub @wojo, X @rwojo); spill v2's block store
design (2,048-token blocks, lineage, the write-at-keep ring, release and fsync
order): BadAd84 (https://github.com/BadAd84),
MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold#118; GLM patch 0110,
MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold#124.
Apply from the checkout root with: git apply 0019-zig-core-cache-tier.patch

